ry and  kickback scandals at one of the  country's most powerful and important  unions the United Auto Workers the UAW  and now the Union President Gary Jones  has unexpectedly taken a paid leave as  he was implicated although not yet  charged in the $700,000 bribery scheme  and leading the way in breaking the  story as he has from the beginning  Robert Snell of the Detroit News Detroit  is a union town and the annual Labor Day  Parade is a big event but the news  coverage this year was driven by the  revelation that the city's most powerful  union leader Gary Jones the President of  the United Auto Workers the UAW is the  subject of an FBI investigation of  alleged corruption a story broken by  Robert Snell the Detroit News there were  protesters union workers protesting you  know decrying the corrupt reports of  corruption that have come out of federal  court Gary Jones marched for part of the  parade declined comment to reporters on  the scene and left before the parade was  finished Snell who covers the federal  courts for the Detroit News first  started working on the UAW corruption  story some two years ago yeah there's a  lot of outrage in some circles they were  the workers fear that this is  confirmation of what they've suspected  that some UAW leaders might just be out  for themselves and not for the average  blue-collar worker so far nine people  have been convicted the latest this week  in federal court in Detroit the Union  has sought to portray the convictions as  not that significant I mean I think  we're fairly well sourced with you know  people close to the investigation and  there hasn't been a challenging of any  of the facts what what they have tried  to focus reporters on is that this is  merely a few bad apples who have been  charged and convicted and that this  doesn't represent a broader crisis for  the UAW but this past week Snell and his  colleagues Brianna novo and Ian  Thibodeaux broke the story that the  union president himself  Jones was part of the investigation with  exclusive coverage of an FBI raid on  Jones's home during the search the  investigators had left the garage door  open at Gary Jones home and one of the  neighbors across the street who was  watching this dramatic federal search  unfold grabbed his binoculars and could  see agents in the garage with the open  garage door counting what this neighbor  described as watts of cash really sort  of a dramatic description of what was  unfolding on the scene today federal  agents descended on the homes of some  top officials with one of the nation's  largest unions the raid on the home of  the UAW president was picked up by news  organizations across the country who had  pretty much ignored the story until now  today FBI Special Agents searching  inside the Canton Michigan home of UAW  president Gary Jones it's been somewhat  of a lonely beat once we broke the news  that the FBI and IRS and Labor  Department were you know investigating  and searching multiple locations around  the country the the coverage mushroomed  you know you started to see the  financial wires and pretty much every  major newspaper Wall Street Journal  start to weigh in and cover the  developments from last week the  allegations are reminiscent of the  blatant corruption at the Teamsters  Union something I covered in my early  days as an NBC News correspondent except  now there are a lot fewer reporters  assigned to cover such stories it's  tough in the newspaper industry with you  know fewer resources but thankfully my  editors have stayed committed to the  story for over two years so it's it's a  matter of you know what we're going to  devote resources to and this is like you  said a union town it's a big story in  Detroit and we've been staying on top of  it for two years plus and there's more  to come you think for sure definitely so  a shout out for Robert still at his  colleagues at the Detroit News for  staying on the story which clearly is  far from over  that's our program for tonight thanks  for joining us we'll see you back here  next week good night
 

